  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nStrong surface high pressure remains in place over southeastern \nCanada. East-northeasterly flow and moisture advection continues \nfrom the Mid-Atlantic states westward towards Indiana. Widespread \nstratus has been in place since yesterday.\n\nAloft, an upper-level low is advancing into Indiana from the west. \nThis feature has allowed the surface wind field to gain cyclonic \ncurvature. Combined with steepening lapse rates aloft, this has lead \nto broad lift within the stratus deck. Drizzle has developed across \nmost of the region as of 3am this morning.\n\nToday and Tonight\n\nDrizzle should continue into the morning hours, before diurnal \nmixing causes it to diminish somewhat. Light rain showers may become \nthe more dominant precipitation mechanism. As such, slight to chance \nPoPs will be carried today. Precipitation chances increase this \nevening into tonight as the upper low settles in. Drizzle may once \nagain become more prominent during the overnight hours.\n\nCeilings will be low today, with cloud bases only 500 to 1000 feet \nabove ground level. Diurnal mixing may allow this to rise to between \n1500 to 2500 feet. Overcast skies are expected through tonight.\n\nThese clouds should keep temperatures suppressed as we've seen in \nprevious days. We will go below guidance with highs only climbing \ninto the low to mid 60s. Lows however will be on the warmer side of \nguidance, due to the insulating effect of the low clouds.\n\nThursday\n\nThings begin to change on Thursday as the upper-level low continues \nsoutheastward. After it departs, surface high pressure to our north \nonce again becomes the dominant feature. Broad subsidence and low-\nlevel dry air advection will help dissipate the stratus. This may \ntake most of the day, unfortunately, so we are still expecting \nmostly cloud skies.\n\nTemperatures a bit warmer than Wednesday are likely, given breaks in \nthe clouds and less rain/drizzle. Highs in the 60s are still the \nmost probable outcome. As for lows, a more dramatic difference is in \nstore for Thursday night. With clearing skies, light winds, \nefficient radiational cooling is anticipated. This should help \ntemperatures drop to the coolest values we've seen since spring. \nLows in the 40s to low 50s are likely across most of the region by \nFriday morning. Patchy fog may be possible as well.\n\nFriday Through Tuesday.\n\nIncreasing subsidence aloft along with weak upper level flow will \nkeep cloud cover at bay Friday into the weekend bringing \ntemperatures closer to normal for this time of the year. Dry air \nadvection continues, with min RH values dropping below 40 percent \nacross south central Indiana which combined with the drier \nconditions of late, may need to be monitored for a low-end fire \ndanger. Near calm winds will help to limit that threat.\n\nLooking further into early next week, temperatures in the lower 80s \nlook to return as the surface flow gradually gains a more southerly \ncomponent along with gradually increasing rain chances towards late \nTuesday ahead of the next system.",
